one in their meld). So two left. Discarding B4 might be safe.\n\n- Play W4: I have W4, but no W5 or W6. Unshown W4 is 3. No one has a meld with W4. So maybe safe.\n\n- Play W3: Unshown W3 is 3. No one has a meld with W3. So safe.\n\nConsidering all, the most dangerous tiles to discard are W7 (risk of kong for player across), B6 (risk for player across's meld), B9 (risk for next player's meld). The safer options are T4, W1, B4, W4, W3.\n\nNow, looking at my hand, I need to decide which tile to discard to help form more melds. For example, if I discard B9, maybe I can focus on forming a B4, B5, B6 chow if I can get B5. But B5 is already in the player across's meld, so not available. Alternatively, if I discard B4, maybe I can form a B4, B5, B6 chow if I can get B5, but again, B5 is already in their meld. So maybe not helpful.\n\nLooking at the W tiles: I have W1, W3, W4, W7. If I can form a chow with W3, W4, W5. But I don't have W5. Or W1, W2, W3. I have W1 and W3, but no W2. So maybe not useful. Alternatively, if I discard W3, maybe I can keep W1, W4, W7 for something else. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T4, which is part of the existing meld, but I have another T4 in the hand. Wait, the meld is [T4, T5, T6], so the T4 in the meld is part of the meld. The T4 in my hand is an extra one. So if I discard T4, it's the one from my hand, not the one in the meld. Since the meld is already formed, maybe it's safe to discard the extra T4. The unshown T4 is 2. The next player has a T4 in their meld, but since they already have it in the meld, maybe it's safe.\n\nSo between the options, discarding T4, W1, B4, W4, W3 are safer. Let's see which one helps my hand more. If I discard T4, I can focus on other tiles. If I discard W1, which is a terminal, maybe I can keep working on other suits. Alternatively, if I discard W3, which is in the W suit, maybe it's better to keep W1, W4, W7 for possible future melds.\n\nWait, I have W1, W3, W4, W7. If I can get a W2, I can form a chow of W1, W2, W3. But W2 is already out (unshown W2 is 0). So no. So W3 is not useful for a chow. Maybe keeping W3 is not helpful. So discarding W3 might be safe.\n\nBut the valid actions include Play W3. Let me check the unshown count for W3: unshown W3 is 3. So three left. So it's safe to discard.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W1, which is a terminal. Since I have a T4 in the meld and B4, maybe focusing on the middle numbers is better. But terminals can be part of patterns like All Terminals or Seven Pairs, but I don't think I have enough here.\n\nConsidering the scoring patterns, if I can form more melds, that's better. My current hand has two T chows. If I can form a B chow or a W chow, that would help. But I need to see which tiles I can get.\n\nIf I discard T4, then I have T4 in the meld and the one in the hand is gone. Maybe that's okay. But I have another T4 in the hand. Wait, no, the meld is [T4, T5, T6], so the T4 in the meld is part of the meld. The T4 in my hand is an extra one. So if I discard it, I lose a T4. But since I have T4 in the meld, maybe it's safe. The unshown T4 is 2, so there are two left. The next player has a T4 in their meld, so maybe they have another one. But since the meld is already formed, discarding T4 is probably safe.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W4, which is part of the W suit. I have W4, but no W5 or W6. So maybe not useful. But if I discard W4, maybe I can focus on other tiles.\n\nAnother angle: the game history of the previous player includes Play W3. So W3 is in play. The unshown W3 is 3, so three left. So discarding W3 is safe.\n\nLooking at the possible actions, I think the safest and most strategic move is to discard T4. Because it's an extra tile in the T suit, which is already part of a meld. By discarding it, I reduce the risk of others completing their melds, and it's a tile that's already in play. The other options like W7 or B6 are riskier due to potential kongs or chows in other players' hands.\n", "output": 